(* Grammar-ambiguity end-to-end tests. Mirrors tests/ambiguity.rs
 * but drives the discrimination through evaluation instead of AST
 * shape: the chosen parse evaluates successfully; every rejected
 * parse would produce a type error or different value. See
 * issue.md ("Grammar ambiguities vs LR(1) limitations") for the
 * full discussion.
 *
 * Where Standard ML has the same ambiguity, the resolution
 * matches SML/NJ — verified directly against the `sml` REPL. *)

Sys.set ("lineWidth", 78);
> val it = () : unit


(* ================================================================
 * A1: postfix dispatch in argument position
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * Input: `f x.y (z)`
 *
 *   Parse A (chosen):  Apply(Apply(f, x.y), z)
 *                      -- `.y` belongs to the argument `x`; the
 *                         whole `x.y` is one arg of `f`.
 *
 *   Parse B (rejected): Apply(Trailing(Apply(f, x), y), z)
 *                      -- `.y` is a trailing method on `Apply(f, x)`.
 *                         This would require `f x` to be a record
 *                         with field y, then apply the result to z.
 *
 * Morel-specific. Standard ML has no `.field` postfix syntax
 * (uses prefix `#field`), so no SML/NJ comparison.
 *
 * Discriminator: with `f : int -> int -> int` and `x : {y:int}`,
 * Parse A succeeds (`f x.y z` = `f 10 5` = 15); Parse B type-errors
 * at `f x` (int function applied to record). *)

val f = fn p => fn q => p + q;
> val f = fn : int -> int -> int
val x = {y = 10};
> val x = {y=10} : {y:int}
f x.y (5);
> val it = 15 : int

(*) Two-step chain via the trailing-method rule:
(*)   cs.complement ().complement () should be
(*)     Apply(Apply(Apply(#complement, Apply(Apply(#complement, cs), ())), ()))
(*) The second `.complement ()` is the trailing method on the
(*) result of the first application.
val cs = {complement = fn () => {complement = fn () => 42}};
> val cs = {complement=fn} : {complement:unit -> {complement:unit -> int}}
cs.complement ().complement ();
> val it = 42 : int


(* ================================================================
 * A2: `if a then b else c d` — dangling application across `else`
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 *   Parse A (chosen):  If(a, b, Apply(c, d))
 *                      -- `c d` is one application forming the
 *                         else clause.
 *
 *   Parse B (rejected): Apply(If(a, b, c), d)
 *                      -- the if-expression is itself the
 *                         function, applied to `d`.
 *
 * Both parses are well-formed grammatically. SML/NJ chooses A;
 * Morel matches. Verified by typing `if true then 1 else g 7;`
 * in SML/NJ — succeeds with `val it = 1 : int` because the else
 * clause `g 7 : int` unifies with the then clause `1 : int`.
 * Parse B would have branches `1 : int` and `g : int -> int`
 * which don't unify — type error.
 *
 * Discriminator: successful evaluation to 1 (then-branch picked)
 * proves Parse A. *)

val g = fn x => x + 100;
> val g = fn : int -> int
if true then 1 else g 7;
> val it = 1 : int

(*) The "false" side picks up `g 7` = 107, again proving the else
(*) clause greedily absorbed the application.
if false then 1 else g 7;
> val it = 107 : int


(* ================================================================
 * A3: `fn p => fn q => 1 | r => 2` — `|` ownership
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 *   Parse A (chosen):  Fn([p => Fn([q => 1, r => 2])])
 *                      -- `| r => 2` is the second arm of the
 *                         INNER fn. Outer fn has one arm.
 *
 *   Parse B (rejected): Fn([p => Fn([q => 1]), r => 2])
 *                      -- `| r => 2` is the second arm of the
 *                         OUTER fn. Inner fn has one arm.
 *
 * SML/NJ chooses A; Morel matches.
 *
 * Type discriminator: under Parse A, the outer fn has signature
 * `'a -> 'b -> int` (a curried 2-arg function). Under Parse B,
 * the outer fn's two arms would have body types `'b -> int` and
 * `int` — they don't unify, so Parse B is a type error.
 *
 * Reversed match order avoids redundancy warnings: `fn 0 => 2 |
 * y => 1` lists the specific case first, so `y` (catch-all) is
 * NOT redundant. *)

val nested = fn x => fn 0 => 2 | y => 1;
> val nested = fn : 'a -> int -> int

(*) Applying twice (curried) — proves Parse A: outer takes one
(*) arg, returns the inner fn, which then takes an int.
nested 99 0;
> val it = 2 : int
nested 99 5;
> val it = 1 : int

(*) The classic redundant-match form. SML/NJ reports the error
(*) on the INNER match-list range (the y/z pair), confirming
(*) Parse A. Morel does the same — the error range below
(*) (1.21-1.27 = the `z => 2` arm) is inside the inner fn, not
(*) at the outer level. *)
fn x => fn y => 1 | z => 2;
> stdIn:1.21-1.27 Error: match redundant
>   raised at: stdIn:1.21-1.27

(*) Forcing Parse B grouping with explicit parens around the
(*) inner fn. Now the outer fn really has two arms (x and z),
(*) and they have incompatible body types (`'a -> int` vs `int`),
(*) producing a type error. (Morel's error wording differs from
(*) SML/NJ's "types of rules do not agree"; the discriminator
(*) here is the existence of a type error, not its phrasing.) *)
fn x => (fn y => 1) | z => 2;
> stdIn:1.1-1.29 Error: Cannot deduce type: conflict: fn(T16, int) vs int
>   raised at: stdIn:1.1-1.29


(*) End ambiguity.smli
